From: BeeWi BC7PW bring-up <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] mt7601sta: wext_notify_event_assoc: build the event payload on the heap

Probe for the fault localised by 0010. wext_notify_event_assoc() fills a
256-byte local buffer and hands its address to RtmpOSWrielessEventSend(),
i.e. a stack pointer escapes into the kernel's WEXT event path. Move the
payload to a heap buffer so no stack address leaves the frame, and free it
once the send returns.

This is deliberately a separate patch from 0010: 0010 adds only printk()
calls and cannot change behaviour, so if the fault clears with both applied
the payload is implicated, and dropping 0011 alone restores it. If the
fault survives, 0010's markers say which half of the function to look at.

Note this is a lifetime/aliasing probe, not a claim that the stack copy
overflows: ReqVarIELen <= IW_CUSTOM_MAX guards that copy.

--- a/common/rt_os_util.c
+++ b/common/rt_os_util.c
@@ -134,7 +134,12 @@
 	IN UCHAR *ReqVarIEs,
 	IN UINT32 ReqVarIELen)
 {
-	char custom[IW_CUSTOM_MAX] = {0};
+	UCHAR *custom = (UCHAR *)kmalloc(IW_CUSTOM_MAX, GFP_ATOMIC);
+	
+	if (custom == NULL)
+		return -1;
+	
+	NdisZeroMemory(custom, IW_CUSTOM_MAX);
 	printk(KERN_ERR "WXTRAP entry len=%u\n", (unsigned)ReqVarIELen);
 
 #if WIRELESS_EXT > 17
@@ -144,6 +149,7 @@
 		printk(KERN_ERR "WXTRAP 1-after-copy len=%u\n", (unsigned)ReqVarIELen);
 		RtmpOSWrielessEventSend(pNetDev, RT_WLAN_EVENT_ASSOC_REQ_IE, -1, NULL,
 								(UCHAR *)custom, ReqVarIELen);
+		kfree(custom);
 		printk(KERN_ERR "WXTRAP 2-after-send\n");
 	}
 	else
